-- title:
--   Descent of an irreducible 2-dimensional representation to a subfield
-- statement:
--   Let $\Omega$ be a field, $V$ an $\Omega$-vector space, $G$ a group, $F$ a subfield of $\Omega$ and $\rho : G \to \mathrm{GL}_\Omega(V)$ a group homomorphism into the $\Omega$-linear automorphisms of $V$. Assume: $V$ has $\Omega$-dimension $2$; every submodule $W \subseteq V$ with $\rho(g)v \in W$ for all $g \in G$ and all $v \in W$ is either $\bot$ or $\top$; for every $g \in G$ the trace of the $\Omega$-linear endomorphism underlying $\rho(g)$ lies in $F$; for every $g \in G$ the determinant of that endomorphism lies in $F$. Assume moreover that there are $g_0 \in G$ and $a \in \Omega$ with $a \in F$ such that $a$ is an eigenvalue of the endomorphism underlying $\rho(g_0)$, and such that this endomorphism is not the scalar $a \cdot \mathrm{id}$. The conclusion is that there exists a basis $b$ of $V$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,2$ such that for every $g \in G$ and all indices $i, j$, the $(i,j)$ entry of the matrix of $\rho(g)$ with respect to $b$ on both sides lies in $F$; that is, $\rho$ is realised by matrices in $\mathrm{M}_2(F)$.
--
--   This is the standard $2$-dimensional descent lemma: an irreducible representation whose traces and determinants lie in a subfield $F$, and which contains one non-scalar element with an $F$-rational eigenvalue, can be conjugated so as to take values in $\mathrm{GL}_2(F)$. No finiteness of $G$, of its image or of $F$, and no algebraic closedness, is assumed. It is used in [`ModularCurve.exists_semisimple_descent_of_trace_det_mem_range_finite`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_semisimple_descent_of_trace_det_mem_range_finite) to realise a residual representation over the subfield generated by its traces and determinants.
